# Highly effective ammonia removal in a series of Brønsted acidic porous polymers: investigation of chemical and structural variations

Although a widely used and important industrial gas, ammonia $(N H_{3})$ is also highly toxic and presents a substantial health and environmental hazard. The development of new materials for the effective capture and removal of ammonia is thus of significant interest. The capture of ammonia at ppm-level concentrations relies on strong interactions between the adsorbent and the gas, as demonstrated in a number of zeolites and metal–organic frameworks with Lewis acidic open metal sites. However, these adsorbents typically exhibit diminished capacity for ammonia in the presence of moisture due to competitive adsorption of water and/or reduced structural stability. In an effort to overcome these challenges, we are investigating the performance of porous polymers functionalized with Brønsted acidic groups, which should possess inherent structural stability and enhanced reactivity towards ammonia in the presence of moisture. Herein, we report the syntheses of six different Brønsted acidic porous polymers exhibiting $-N H_{3}C l,$ $-C O_{2}H.$ $-S O_{3}H.$ , and $-P O_{3}H_{2}$ groups and featuring two different network structures with respect to interpenetration. We further report the low- and high-pressure $N H_{3}$ uptake in these materials, as determined under dry and humid conditions using gas adsorption and breakthrough measurements. Under dry conditions, it is possible to achieve $N H_{3}$ capacities as high as 2 mmol ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ at 0.05 mbar (50 ppm) equilibrium pressure, while breakthrough saturation capacities of greater than 7 mmol ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ are attainable under humid conditions. Chemical and structural variations deduced from these measurements also revealed an important interplay between acidic group spatial arrangement and $N H_{3}$ uptake, in particular that interpenetration can promote strong adsorption even for weaker Brønsted acidic functionalities. In situ infrared spectroscopy provided further insights into the mechanism of $N H_{3}$ adsorption, revealing a proton transfer between ammonia and acidic sites as well as strong hydrogen bonding interactions in the case of the weaker carboxylic acid-functionalized polymer. These findings highlight that an increase of acidity or porosity does not necessarily correspond directly to increased $N H_{3}$ capacity and advocate for the development of more fine-tuned design principles for efficient $N H_{3}$ capture under a range of concentrations and conditions.
## Introduction
Solid-state adsorbents such as metal–organic frameworks1 and porous polymers2 are intriguing classes of materials that have shown signicant advancement over the past two decades. Given the exibility in their design and synthesis, the physical and chemical properties of these materials can be ne-tuned3 for a specic application and in particular they have been extensively investigated for applications in gas storage and chemical separations.4 In the context of gas capture or separations, it is important to have preferential binding sites with a large enthalpy of adsorption to achieve selective and efficient separation of ppm-level contaminants from the rest of the mixture.5
One example where this is of particular importance is in the removal of ammonia $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)$ from air.6 Ammonia is one of the most highly produced chemicals, and is widely utilized in many essential segments of industry.7 On the other hand, ammonia is also highly toxic and poses signicant health and environmental concerns.8,9 For example, CAL-OSHA has limited shortterm ammonia exposure to levels as low as $35~\mathrm{ppm}$ while an eight-hour time-weighted average exposure has been limited to 25 ppm.10 Effective protection mechanisms against excess ammonia exposure are therefore highly desired in many industrial settings, as well as for military applications. Another potentially interesting application is the removal of residual ammonia in ${\bf N H}_{3}$ -based fuel cells aer the gas decomposes to hydrogen.11 Indeed, even a small amount of ammonia slip could poison the catalyst and acidic membrane in a fuel cell, and adsorbents that can capture ammonia efficiently at low concentrations could play an important role in the advancement of this technology. In addition to the selective removal of ammonia, porous materials have the potential to exhibit highcapacity storage of ammonia under ambient or high pressures, which would provide a safer alternative to compressed liquid ammonia (10 bar, $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) for transportation and recycling applications.
Ammonia can behave as both a Lewis base and a Brønsted base, and therefore porous materials decorated with Lewis or Brønsted acidic sites are promising targets for capture at particularly low concentrations.12 Certain metal–organic frameworks exhibit exposed metal cations, which serve as Lewis acid sites that strongly adsorb ammonia, and these frameworks have been shown to take up high quantities of ammonia at low concentrations under dry conditions.6c,13 However, the capacities of these materials are generally signicantly diminished in the presence of moisture, a scenario that reects actual practical operating conditions for ammonia scrubbers.6c,13 Such behavior can be attributed to the competition between water and ammonia molecules for Lewis acid sites, as well as to the instability of some of the investigated frameworks under humid conditions. To overcome these problems, a water-stable framework, UiO-66, functionalized with a series of Brønsted acidic groups $\left(-\mathbf{OH}\right.$ , $\begin{array}{r}{-\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2},}\end{array}$ ， $-\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ , $-S\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}{\dot{\mathbf{\Omega}}}_{.}$ ) was recently investigated for $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ capture.6g Although considerable improvement in ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ capacity was achieved using these materials, there was also a signicant reduction in porosity upon functionalization with bulkier groups $-\mathbf{CO_{2}H}$ and $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , which hindered the accessibility and complete utilization of the acid sites. More recently, incorporation into a polymer membrane has been demonstrated to impart enhanced stability to the framework HKUST-1 under humid conditions, without diminishing $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ capacity.14
Porous polymers have been investigated to a lesser degree in the context of low-pressure ${\bf N H}_{3}$ capture.15 In contrast to metal– organic frameworks, porous polymers inherently exhibit16 a high chemical and thermal stability, due to their covalent backbone and accordingly should not suffer from degradation in the presence of ammonia and/or moisture. Furthermore, a diverse toolbox of synthetic organic chemistry allows not only the preparation of polymers with desired porosity (surface area and pore size) but also the incorporation of Brønsted acidic groups in a facile manner. Recently, we demonstrated that a three-dimensional porous polymer functionalized with carboxylic acid groups exhibits a high ammonia uptake of $3.15~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at an equilibrium concentration of ${\sim}500~\mathrm{ppm}.^{1}$ 7 The spatial arrangement of multiple carboxylic acids in this material—most likely a result of its multiply-interpenetrated structure—leads to the cooperative reactivity, which in turn creates strong adsorption sites for ammonia at low pressures. Additionally, a sulfonic acid-functionalized porous polymer, prepared from a non-interpenetrated, high-surface area porous aromatic framework (PAF-1), exhibited a lower ammonia capacity of $1.54~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at ${\sim}900~\mathrm{ppm}$ , in spite of its stronger Brønsted acidity. This behavior was attributed to the presence of isolated and non-interacting acidic groups due to the noninterpenetrated structure of the material. Such a striking difference between these two polymers prompted us to further investigate the interplay between Brønsted acidity and polymer structure in a systematic manner.
Herein, we report the synthesis and characterization of six distinct Brønsted acidic porous polymers incorporating $\mathbf{-NH}_{3}\mathbf{Cl}$ , $-\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H}$ , $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , and $-\mathrm{PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ groups (Fig. 1a) and describe their low- and high-pressure ${\bf N H}_{3}$ uptake behavior under dry and humid conditions using static gas adsorption and dynamic breakthrough measurements. Through successful incorporation of various acidic groups into non-interpenetrated18 (P1) and interpenetrated19 (P2) frameworks (Fig. 1b), we were able to investigate the impact of acidic group strength and their spatial arrangement on the overall ${\bf N H}_{3}$ uptake in a three-dimensionally conned environment. For the best performing materials, we also present the results of in situ infrared spectroscopic characterization of the interactions between ammonia and Brønsted acid sites.
## Results and discussion
## Synthesis
The Brønsted acidic porous polymers reported in this work were synthesized following either a postsynthetic functionalization strategy starting from PAF-1 or a de novo approach—i.e., rst installing Brønsted acidic moieties on one of the monomers and then carrying out a Suzuki coupling polymerization. Preparations for the polymers $\mathbf{P1-NH_{3}C l}$ (also known as BPP-2) and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ (also known as BPP-7) were recently reported,17 while the synthesis of $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ (also known as $\mathbf{PPN-6-SO_{3}H})$ was achieved following a literature procedure.20
Porous polymers $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-NH}_{3}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ were synthesized (Fig. 1a) through the Suzuki coupling route. Precursors 1a and 1b were produced starting from 2-amino-1,4-dibromobenzene and 1,4-dibromobenzene, respectively (see ESI†). The tert-butyloxycarbonyl (Boc) protection21 of the amino group in 2-amino1,4-dibromobenzene using di-tert-butyl dicarbonate was followed by Miyaura borylation to afford 1a. The sulfonation of 1,4- dibromobenzene using chlorosulfonic acid $\mathrm{(ClSO_{3}H)}$ provided 2,5-dibromobenzenesulfonyl chloride.22 A neopentyl group was chosen as the protecting group in this instance, due to the stability of the resulting sulfonic ester under basic Suzuki–
Miyaura coupling conditions, as well as to its ease of removal aer polymerization.23 Esterication of 2,5-dibromobenzenesulfonyl chloride with neopentyl alcohol and subsequent Miyaura borylation delivered 1b. Suzuki polymerization of 1a and 1b with the tetrahedral precursor tetrakis(4-bromophenyl) methane using Buchwald's precatalyst (SPhos Pd G2) yielded polymers P2-NHBoc and ${\bf P}2{\cdot}{\bf S}{\bf O}_{3}{\bf N}{\bf e}{\bf o}$ , respectively. The utilization of a $4\mathrm{{MHCl}}$ solution in 1,4-dioxane enabled the removal of the Boc group and protonation of the amino group simultaneously to yield the nal polymer, $\mathrm{\bfP}2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\bfN}\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfCl}$ . In the case of P2- ${\tt S O}_{3}{\tt N e o}$ , hydrolysis of the neopentyl group was carried out using ${\bf N a N}_{3}$ in DMSO and subsequent acidication with 6 M HCl resulted in the polymer $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ .
Similar to previously reported syntheses of P1- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{3}\mathbf{Cl}$ and P1- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , the polymer $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ was prepared through postsynthetic modication (Fig. 1a). The parent PAF-1 was synthesized following the original procedure,24 while the chloromethylation of PAF-1 to afford the intermediate PAF-1- $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}$ was achieved as reported previously.5b The conversion of PAF- $\mathsf{I-C H_{2}C l}$ into the ethyl phosphonate polymer P1- $\mathbf{\cdotPO}_{3}\mathbf{Et}_{2}$ was accomplished through a Michaelis–Arbuzov reaction in the presence of neat triethyl phosphite, $\mathbf{P(OEt)}_{3}$ , at elevated temperatures. The mild hydrolysis of the phosphonate ester groups into phosphonic acid moieties was readily accomplished using ${\bf M}{\bf e}_{3}\mathrm{SiBr}$ , affording the polymer $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ in a quantitative manner.
## Polymer characterization
The formation of porous polymers incorporating protected Brønsted acidic groups and their subsequent hydrolysis was conrmed by Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy (Fig. $S7{\mathrm{-}}S9{\dagger}$ ) and solid-state ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ and $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectroscopy (Fig. S10–S19†). The FTIR spectra of $\mathbf{P1-NH_{3}C l}$ , $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ , and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ were found to be in accordance with previously reported data.17,20 The formation of $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{Et}_{2}$ was conrmed by the appearance of characteristic $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=}\mathbf{O}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P-O-C}$ stretching bands at 1240 and $960{-}1050~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. The efficiency of this conversion was also reected in the elemental analysis data (Table S1†). The initial chlorine content of $16.2\%$ in PAF-1- $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}$ was reduced to $0.6\%$ in the subsequent phosphonate ester polymer. Moreover, hydrolysis of $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{Et}_{2}$ to $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ resulted in a shi in the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=O}$ stretching band to $1140\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the appearance of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P-O-H}$ bands in the range of $930{-}1000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Complete hydrolysis of ester groups was also evidenced by the absence of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P-O-C}$ stretching bands. Similarly, complete removal of the Boc groups in $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-NH}_{3}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ was unambiguously demonstrated by the disappearance of a $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O}$ stretching band at $1713~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ as well as the emergence of a broad $\mathbf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ band centered around $3345~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . In the case of $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ , absorption bands at $960~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (S–O–C stretching) and at 1178 and 1350 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\scriptstyle\mathbf{S}=\mathbf{O}$ stretching) were replaced by bands at 996 and 1020 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (S–OH) and $1150~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(\mathrm{{{S=O}}})$ , respectively. Finally, the disappearance of $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ stretching bands in the $2900{-}3000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ region also indicated the removal of neopentyl groups.
The extent of removal for each protecting group and the structural integrity of $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{{P2}\mathrm{{-NH_{3}C l}}}$ , $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ , and P2- $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ were further examined by magic angle spinning (MAS) $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopy and cross-polarization magic angle spinning (CP/MAS) $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectroscopy. Acidic protons appear as sharp, narrow peaks, especially in the cases of $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , P2- $\mathrm{so}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , and $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ in the solid-state MAS ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra, reecting the high mobility of acidic protons in these materials (Fig. S12, S16 and $^{\mathrm{S18\dagger}}$ ). The disappearance of the protons associated with protecting groups further suggested high conversion efficiencies and purities of the nal Brønsted acidic porous polymers. The CP/MAS $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectra also conrmed the hydrolysis of protecting groups by revealing an absence of chemical shis in the range of $10{-}40~\mathrm{ppm}$ , for aliphatic carbon atoms on alkyl groups as well as in the $60{-}80~\mathrm{ppm}$ , region for aliphatic carbon atoms attached to oxygen. Broad chemical shis in the 110–170 ppm region are ascribed to the presence of aromatic subunits in both protected and nal acidic polymers, which also conrm the structural integrity and stability of these polymers under harsh hydrolysis conditions. Scanning electron microscopy images further revealed that the P1 polymers consistently display a sphere-like morphology, while all P2 polymer particles exhibit sheet-like features (Fig. S20 and S21†).
## Surface area and pore size distribution
Surface area and porosity analyses were carried out using nitrogen gas adsorption isotherms collected at $77~\mathrm{K}$ Pore size distributions were calculated from the adsorption branch of isotherms employing a quenched solid-state DFT (QSDFT) model, which takes surface heterogeneity into account and assumes the presence of a mixture of slit, cylindrical, and spherical pores. All polymers display type I reversible isotherms, as typically observed for microporous materials (Fig. $S24\mathrm{-}S29\dagger.$ ) and the overall results from these measurements are summarized in Table 1. The BET surface areas of $\mathbf{P1-NH_{3}C l}$ , $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ , and $\mathrm{P1-PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ were found to be 975, 1220, and $835~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Since the parent material PAF-1 is considered to be a non-interpenetrated framework owing to its high surface area and expected pore size of ${\sim}11$ A˚,18b postsynthetic functionalization to afford the P1 polymers is expected to preserve this architecture, thus rendering Brønsted acidic sites relatively well-isolated. In spite of the expanded repeating unit length for the P2 polymers (terphenyl instead of biphenyl), the BET surface areas are lower overall than those of the P1 polymers (Table 1). The narrower pore sizes for the P2 polymers, in the range $6{-}10\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ , also suggests a signicant degree of interpenetration and that the Brønsted acidic groups are most likely in close proximity to one another. The relatively high BET surface area for $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-NH}_{3}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ $(980~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ in this series might be attributed to the presence of the sterically encumbering Boc groups used in the synthesis of the P2-NHBoc precursor, which could reduce the degree of interpenetration.25
It should be noted that the total pore volumes for P1 polymers $(>0.5\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ are consistently higher than those for P2 polymers $\left(<0.5\mathrm{\cm}^{3}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}\right)$ , reecting the inuence of interpenetration in the latter frameworks. The microporosity of each sample was also further conrmed by $t\cdot$ -plot curves (Table 1). All polymers exhibit pore size distributions mainly in the micropore range, while a closer inspection and comparison of pore size distributions for P1 and P2 polymers carrying similar Brønsted acidic groups further reveals some structural differences (Fig. ${\bf S}30\mathrm{-}{\bf S}32\dagger.$ ). For instance, although the pore size distributions for $\mathbf{P1-NH_{3}C l}$ and $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-NH}_{3}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ appear similar, the total volume contribution from pores smaller than $15\mathrm{~\AA~}$ is slightly larger in $\mathrm{\bfP}2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\bfN}\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfCl}$ . This difference becomes more obvious for the polymers comprising $-S{\bf O}_{3}{\bf H}$ moieties; indeed, while the pore width maxima centered around 6 and $9\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ for P1- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ are associated with nearly equal pore volume contributions, the major pore volume contribution in $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ arises from the pores centered around ${\sim}6.5\mathring\mathrm{A}$ . In the case of P $\mathbf{\left.\mathbf{l}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{PO}_{3}H_{2}\right.}$ and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ , both materials exhibit a pore-width maximum at ${\sim}6\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ as well as a pore size distribution in the range $7{-}10\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , although the overall contribution of $6\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ pores is larger for the interpenetrated polymer $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ , while the 7–10 A˚ pores contribute more to the pore volume for $\mathrm{P1-PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Even though these two polymers possess different Brønsted acidic groups, their pore size distributions are in excellent agreement with the anticipated structural features based upon interpenetration.
## $\mathbf{NH}_{3}$ adsorption
Aer thermally degassing the polymers under vacuum, ${\bf N H}_{3}$ uptake was investigated at pressures of up to 1 bar at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ under dry conditions (Fig. 2). As mentioned earlier, the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ isotherms for $\mathrm{P1-NH_{3}C l}$ , $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ , and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ were recently reported.17 For the sake of a clear discussion and comparison in this section, these isotherms have also been included with those newly collected for $\mathrm{P1-PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-NH}_{3}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ , and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ .
Among the P1 polymers, $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ and $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ exhibit a signicant improvement over $\mathbf{P1-NH_{3}C l}$ at low pressures, consistent with the stronger Brønsted acidity of the sulfonic and phosphonic acid groups. For example, at 0.5 mbar $(500\ \mathrm{ppm})$ , $\mathbf{P1-NH_{3}C l}$ displays an excess uptake of $0.37\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ compared to 1.35 and $3.3\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}$ , for $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ and $\mathbf{P1-PO_{3}H_{2}}$ , respectively (Fig. 2a). Notably, $\mathrm{P1-PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ shows an uptake of 18.7 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at 1 bar, which represents a substantial improvement over the uptakes of 11.2 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{P1-NH_{3}C l}$ and $12.1\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ at the same pressure. It should be noted that even though phosphonic acid is less acidic than sulfonic acid, $\mathrm{P}\mathrm{1-PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ exhibits a steeper uptake in the lowpressure region of the isotherm and higher uptake at low and high pressures under dry conditions. The diacidic nature of this functional group, which increases the total number of acidic sites, and the smaller surface area and pore volume of the associated polymer most likely contribute to the enhanced adsorption under dry conditions. As expected within the series of interpenetrated P2 polymers, $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ outperform $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-NH}_{3}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ in terms of low-pressure ${\bf N H}_{3}$ uptake (Fig. 2b). The ${\bf N H}_{3}$ capacity at 0.5 mbar is $2.25\mathrm{mmolg}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ and 3.15 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ , compared to $0.27\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for P2- $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ . Moreover, despite the weaker acidity of the carboxylic acid groups compared to sulfonic acids, the higher carboxylic acid density (6.5 compared with $2.8\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}$ sulfonic acid groups) enables greater $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ uptake at low pressures in P2- $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}.$ —a comparison that also suggests the formation of strong binding sites with multiple weak acidic groups. At 1 bar, P2- $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ display similar $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ uptakes of 16.3 and 16.1 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, whereas $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ has a lower uptake of $13.1\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
Perhaps more interestingly and relevant to permissible exposure limits, we now compare the uptake properties of these materials at a signicantly lower ${\bf N H}_{3}$ concentration of 50 ppm (0.05 mbar, see right panels in Fig. 2a and b). At this pressure, $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ exhibit a capacity of 0.01 and $1.79\mathrm{~mmol}$ $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Given the lower $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ loading in $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ (Table 1), it is clear that the spatial distribution and proximity of neighboring acidic groups within the pores is also an important factor that can substantially inuence the ammonia affinity of the material. On the other hand, at $50~\mathrm{ppm}~\mathrm{P1\mathrm{-}P O_{3}H_{2}}$ and P2- $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ display similarly high ${\bf N H}_{3}$ uptakes of 2.03 and $1.62\mathrm{mmol}$ $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The improvement in the case of $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO_{2}H}$ clearly demonstrates that the cooperative action of multiple weaker acidic sites can outperform strong acidic sites that are more isolated. As for $\mathrm{P1-PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , the enhanced ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ uptake relative to $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ is likely a result of: (i) a higher density of acidic sites, (ii) the bulkiness and exibility of the $-\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ groups compared to $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups, and/or (iii) a smaller surface area and pore volume. All of these factors render the acidic sites more proximal in $\mathrm{P1-PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ than in $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ .
Another plausible explanation for the increased ${\bf N H}_{3}$ uptake in the cases of $\mathbf{P1-PO_{3}H_{2}}$ , $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ , and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ can be drawn from some recent computational work.12d In this study of the interaction of ammonia with isolated Brønsted acidic groups, it was established that protonation of ammonia depends on the dielectric constant of the medium. Given the presence of more isolated sulfonic acid groups in $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ , it is possible that a low local polarity contributes to decreased ammonia affinity at these sites for concentrations as low as 50 ppm. Network interpenetration in the case of $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ and the reduced pore volume in $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ could, however, create a local dielectric polarization around each acidic site in the pores and therefore lead to stronger interactions with ammonia and enhanced capacities. Most notably, the performance of these latter materials at 50 ppm of dry ammonia is comparable to that of 5A zeolite (1.86 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $58\mathrm{\ppm})$ and 13X zeolite $\left(1.74\mathrm{~mmol~g}^{-1}\right.$ at $41\ \mathrm{ppm}$ ) and is signicantly higher than sulfonated polymeric resin Amberlyst 15 $\mathrm{0.38~mmol~g^{-1}}$ at 71 ppm).26
The polymer acidity within the P1 and P2 series, as well as between those with the same functional group (e.g., $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ and $\mathbf{P}2{\cdot}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{\dot{\Sigma}},$ , was compared aer plotting gravimetric ${\bf N H}_{3}$ isotherms with respect to the number of acidic functionalities $\mathrm{\left(mmol_{NH_{3}}\ m m m o l_{a c i d}-^{1}\right)}$ determined by elemental analysis. The absolute pressure corresponding to the capture of one equivalent of ammonia per acid site was found to correlate well with the acid strength of functional groups within P1 and P2 (Fig. S33 and ${\bf S}34{\dagger}$ ). It should be noted that this pressure does not necessarily correspond to complete saturation of acidic sites, since $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ adsorption is typically complex6d and capture mechanisms through strong hydrogen bonding and/or van der Waals interactions in small pores cannot be ruled out. Nevertheless, such a comparison is still useful for comparison of the overall binding affinity of the polymers towards ammonia. The abovementioned $1:1$ interaction is apparent at 12 mbar for P1- $\mathrm{PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , 35 mbar for $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ , and 380 mbar for $\mathrm{P1-NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ (Fig. $\mathbf{S}33\dagger.$ ), and the lower pressure observed for $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ compared to $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ can be attributed to reasons discussed above. The $1:1$ interaction occurs at 2 mbar for $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ 12 mbar for $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ , and 40 mbar for $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-N}\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ (Fig. ${\bf S}34\dagger_{.}$ , correlating well with the Brønsted acidity of individual sites in these materials. Remarkably, comparison of these pressures between polymers with the same Brønsted acidic functionality reveals the importance of structural features on the overall acidity. For instance, $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-NH}_{3}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ exhibits a saturation pressure of 40 mbar compared to a much larger 380 mbar for $\mathbf{P1-NH_{3}C l},$ thus demonstrating that acidic site proximity due to interpenetration can enhance site acidity. Likewise, a similar effect is apparent for $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ and $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ , in which the corresponding pressures are 2 and 35 mbar, respectively.
## Breakthrough measurements
In order to gain further understanding of the ${\bf N H}_{3}$ adsorption in these Brønsted acidic porous polymers and to investigate their removal efficiency under practical conditions, we carried out dynamic microbreakthrough measurements6c,13,27 under dry and humid conditions at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ . The breakthrough curves for the P1 and P2 materials are shown in Fig. 3 and the corresponding capacities upon saturation are summarized in Table 2. The breakthrough curves are plotted on a weighted mass basis to correct for the density of the materials and the partial pressure of ammonia in the feed stream ( $\cdot\mathrm{\Delta}C_{0},$ $2000\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{m}^{-3}.$ ) was approximately 2.8 mbar.
Under dry conditions, the trends in breakthrough saturation capacity and uptake are in excellent agreement with those obtained from gas adsorption measurements (Fig. 3a and b, solid
We also sought to establish whether the ${\bf N H}_{3}$ uptake in these materials is reversible, and accordingly monitored the shape of desorption curves obtained upon purging with dry air aer ammonia saturation. In the case of the P1 polymers, broad desorption curves suggest a gradual elution of ammonia, while a signicant amount of adsorbed ammonia is released from P1- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ and $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . In contrast, $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ retain higher amounts of the gas, as evidenced by their steeper desorption curves. This greater ammonia retention of the interpenetrated P2 polymers can be explained by the presence of a more conned environment around the acidic groups and enhanced interactions with ammonia as a result of the framework interpenetration.
lines). For all polymers, the breakthrough curves exhibit a steep slope that suggests that the ammonia capture process is not diffusion controlled. As expected, the breakthrough of ammonia in $\mathbf{P1-NH_{3}C l}$ and $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-NH}_{3}\mathbf{Cl}$ , with the weakest Brønsted acidic functionality, occurs much earlier than for the other polymers carrying sulfonic, phosphonic, or carboxylic acid groups within the same series. Also in agreement with the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ adsorption isotherms, $\mathrm{P1-PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ display highest uptake capacities of 5.2 and $6.7\:\mathrm{mmol}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, under dry breakthrough conditions.
Notably, in the presence of $80\%$ relative humidity (RH), the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ saturation capacities improve for all polymers when compared with capacities under dry conditions (Fig. 3a and b, dashed lines). The overall capacity increase in the P1 polymers is greater than for the P2 polymers in the presence of water. For example, $\mathbf{P1-NH_{3}C l}$ and $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ display saturation capacities of 2.0 and 8.1 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, corresponding to more than twice their dry capacities, and $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ exhibits a humid capacity of $7.2\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , nearly 1.5 times the uptake under dry conditions. The saturation capacities increase only marginally for $\mathbf{P}2\mathrm{-NH}_{3}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}$ , $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ , and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ . Such differences in uptake improvement between the two polymer families can be ascribed to the larger pore volumes of the P1 polymers, which in turn can potentially accommodate more water molecules. Indeed, $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ has the largest total pore volume and exhibits the most signicant improvement in $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ uptake. Water adsorption experiments further conrmed a high water uptake of ${\sim}40~\mathrm{mmol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ at $80\%$ RH (Fig. S41†). Provided a material is stable under humid conditions, it has previously been found that the presence of water can enhance ${\bf N H}_{3}$ capacity. $^{13,15b,c,28}$ In these cases, dissolution of ammonia in water has been identied as an ammonia retention mechanism.29 Furthermore, since the polymers presented here are decorated with polar Brønsted acidic groups, it is possible that: (i) the presence of water facilitates proton transfer between acid sites and ammonia molecules and (ii) dissolution of ammonia is enhanced due to the formation of hydrogen bonding interactions between the polar adsorbent surface and ammonia.30 These possible mechanisms highlight the advantage of Brønsted acidic sites over Lewis acidic sites in the context of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ capture.
We note that while $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ displays a broad desorption curve under humid conditions, the curve for $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ is rather steep (Fig. 3a, dashed lines). Although ammonia dissolution is a weak retention mechanism and captured ammonia can be released easily upon purging with air,29 it appears that the acidity enhancement afforded by water is more pronounced for $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ . On the other hand, ammonia is more strongly held in the P2 polymers, for instance both $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{H}$ and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO_{2}H}$ exhibit steep desorption curves (Fig. 3b, dashed lines) with a signicant ammonia retention. Therefore, depending on whether a high saturation or retention capacity is desired, the polymer structure and chemical features can be adjusted to tune the performance. Based upon humid breakthrough experiments, $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ , $\mathrm{P1-PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ are identied as the best performing materials with saturation capacities of greater than $\mathrm{7~mmol~g^{-1}}$ . Importantly, this value surpasses the capacities of a number of other porous polymers (Table $\mathbf{S}3\dagger.$ ). Moreover, the ${\bf N H}_{3}$ capacities of the Brønsted acidic polymers reported herein are also commensurate with one of the best performing metal–organic frameworks, HKUST-1, which exhibits $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ loadings of 6.6 and 8.9 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ under dry and humid conditions, respectively.13 However, this framework degrades in the presence of moisture and displays diminished $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ uptake aer the initial cycle of exposure unless it is embedded14 within a polymer membrane. In addition to enhanced uptakes, high structural stabilities of $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ , P1- $\mathrm{PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO_{2}H}$ under humid conditions are highly advantageous in terms of recyclability and reusability of these materials in an ${\bf N H}_{3}$ capture process.31
## In situ FTIR spectroscopy
We sought to further probe the interaction of ammonia with Brønsted acidic groups utilizing in situ infrared spectroscopy. Experiments were carried out on $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ , $\mathbf{P1-PO}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , and P2- $\mathbf{CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ at ambient temperature and at an equilibrium $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ pressure of 3 mbar, corresponding to the partial pressure of ammonia in our breakthrough experiments. Notably, spectral changes were observed at around $1450~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for all three polymers, associated with the formation of the ammonium cation $\big(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\big),^{32}$ 2 as well as changes corresponding to deprotonated species of the Brønsted acidic sites. Thus, at this coverage, a proton transfer mechanism appears to be involved in ${\bf N H}_{3}$ uptake for these different acidic moieties. Spectral changes were also monitored upon desorption to assess the reversibility of ammonia binding.
We note that the parent material, PAF-1, does not exhibit any substantial spectral changes upon exposure to ammonia (Fig. ${\bf S}37\dagger$ ). The initial spectrum is dominated by the typical features of this aromatic framework, and upon $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{3}}$ adsorption the spectral changes all corresponded to those associated with the roto-vibrational prole of gaseous ammonia.
The spectrum of activated $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ exhibits a series of very intense and sharp absorption bands in the $800{-}1800~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ spectral range, which are ascribed to the vibrational modes of the aromatic structure and sulfonic acid group (Fig. 4a, red curve). In particular, the signals at 1370, 1095, and $895~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are generated by out-of-phase $\left(\nu_{\mathrm{as}}\right)$ and in-phase $\left(\nu_{s}\right)$ stretching vibrations of the $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ group, and the OH bending mode of the $\mathrm{\bullet}$ OH moiety, respectively.32 Aer equilibrating the sample with 3 mbar of ammonia, important spectral modications become apparent (Fig. 4a, blue curve). A new broad signal emerges in the $1440{-}1490\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ region as a consequence of the formation of ammonium ions and overlaps with one of the peaks resulting from aromatic ring vibrations at $1465~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The absorption bands assigned to $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ and S–OH moieties in the activated material also disappear and two strong signals appear at 1035 and $1225~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ due to $\nu_{\mathrm{s}}$ and $\nu_{\mathrm{as}}$ modes of the newly formed sulfonate $\left(-\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{3}^{-}\right)$ group.32 All of these spectral changes indicate that proton transfer to ammonia indeed occurs at 3 mbar. Aer ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ adsorption, the sample was evacuated for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at beam temperature (residual pressure $<10^{-4}$ mbar) to evaluate the reversibility of the proton transfer (Fig. 4a, green curve). The aforementioned absorption bands at 1035, 1225, and $1485\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ remain almost unchanged upon desorption, demonstrating the high stability of the generated ammonium ion. Additionally, the $\nu_{\mathrm{as}}(\mathbf{SO}_{2})$ and $\nu_{\mathrm{s}}(\mathrm{SO}_{2})$ modes of the sulfonic acid moieties could not be restored, even aer prolonged outgassing.
Spectra collected for in situ ammonia-dosed on $\mathrm{P1-PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ suggested a similar proton transfer between ammonia and phosphonic acid groups within the polymer. The activated spectrum of $\mathrm{P}\mathrm{1-PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ exhibits typical features, in particular, strong and broad signals located at around 1000 and $1200\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and corresponding to $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P-O(H)}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=O}$ stretching vibrations, respectively (Fig. 4b, red curve).32 Upon dosing with ammonia, the $\delta\big(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\big)$ mode of the ammonium ion clearly appears as a broad component at $\sim1450~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , while the P–O and $\scriptstyle\mathbf{P=O}$ stretching modes are deeply perturbed, indicating deprotonation of one or both acidic protons (Fig. 4b, blue curve). Interestingly, outgassing following $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ adsorption results in a partial restoration of the spectral features of the activated sample (Fig. 4b, green curve).
The spectrum of activated $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO_{2}H}$ exhibits two broad signals at 1688 and $1393~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , which we assign to $\scriptstyle\nu(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O})$ stretching and $\delta\mathrm{(C-OH)}$ bending vibrations of the carboxylic acid group, respectively (Fig. 4c, red curve).32 Exposure of the sample to 3 mbar of ammonia leads to immediate appearance of the $\delta\big(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\big)$ mode at $\sim1458~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Fig. 4c, blue curve). Moreover, the $\scriptstyle\nu(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O})$ and $\delta\mathrm{(C-OH)}$ band intensities decrease, and this change is accompanied by the appearance of two new bands at 1525 and $1378~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ that can be ascribed to $\nu_{\mathrm{as}}(\mathrm{OCO})$ and $\nu_{\mathrm{{s}}}(\mathrm{{OCO})}$ modes of the carboxylate $\left(-\mathbf{CO_{2}}^{-}\right)$ group, respectively.32 Notably, in contrast to what was observed for $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ and $\mathbf{P1-PO_{3}H_{2}}$ , for $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ , the proton transfer to ammonia seems to be quite reversible (Fig. 4c, green curve). Indeed, aer outgassing the ammonia-exposed sample, the $\delta\big(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\big)$ and $\nu(\mathrm{OCO})$ bands decrease dramatically and the characteristic signals of the carboxylic acid groups are partially restored.
Another unique feature exhibited by $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ upon ammonia exposure is the noticeable change in the scattering prole of the whole spectrum (Fig. ${\bf S40\dagger}$ ). The spectrum displays an evident increase in the absorption prole below $3000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , indicative of very strong hydrogen bonding. In conjunction with the partially reversible appearance of the ammonium and carboxylate signals, this feature is indicative of the presence of a hesitating proton with high mobility—i.e., the proton uctuates between carboxylic acid and ammonia.33 Importantly, this observation sheds further light on the enhanced $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ adsorption capacity of $\mathbf{P}2\mathbf{-CO}_{2}\mathbf{H}$ over $\mathbf{P1-SO_{3}H}$ at low pressures, as discussed earlier. In spite of a weaker acidity, the high density of carboxylic acid groups in an interpenetrated structure creates strong binding sites for ammonia as well as a stabilizing polar environment for ammonium ions.
